  2: {
    title: 'The Sound of Something New', skill: 'listening',
    goal: 'I can understand creative feedback and distinguish a small revision from a complete change.',
    intro: 'The next morning, Maya hears Kai’s recording of Paper Skyline, a pop song about chasing dreams in a big city. She likes the melody but wants a stronger chorus. The engineer suggests a stronger bass line. Kai worries about losing the song’s identity. They try different arrangements, and he likes the revised version. Feedback begins to feel more like collaboration.',
    warmup: 'How can you suggest a change while recognising what already works?',
    expressions: [
      ['build on what works', 'means to improve something while keeping its successful parts.', 'Maya wants to <strong>build on what works</strong> in the song.'],
      ['bring something out', 'means to make a quality easier to notice or hear.', 'A stronger arrangement could <strong>bring the chorus out</strong>.'],
      ['stay true to something', 'means to keep the main character, meaning or values of it.', 'Kai wants to <strong>stay true to the song</strong>.'],
      ['be open to feedback', 'means to be willing to consider comments that could improve your work.', 'Trying another version helps Kai <strong>be open to feedback</strong>.'],
    ],
    discussion: 'II. You are Maya. Your tutor is Kai. Explain a possible improvement without asking him to lose the song’s identity.', followup: 'Which words show that a suggestion is limited rather than a request to replace the whole song?',
    tips: [
      { rule: 'Acknowledge a strength before suggesting a focused change.', examples: ['The melody is memorable. Could we give the chorus more impact?', 'The song has a clear identity. Let’s try a different arrangement.'] },
      { rule: 'Use not necessarily to correct an assumption without rejecting the person’s concern.', examples: ['Not necessarily. I mean a stronger arrangement, not a new song.', 'Feedback does not necessarily mean giving up your vision.'] },
    ],
    apply: { situation: 'You are Kai. Listen to Maya’s feedback after the first recording.', text: 'Kai, the melody of Paper Skyline is memorable. I want to build on what works, not replace the whole song. The chorus could have more impact. The engineer thinks a stronger bass line might bring it out. I understand that you want to stay true to the song. Let’s try a different arrangement and compare the versions. Being open to feedback does not mean accepting every suggestion. We can listen together before deciding what to keep.', questions: [['What is Maya mainly proposing?', 'Try a stronger arrangement while keeping the song’s identity.'], ['What does she already like?', 'The memorable melody.'], ['What does the engineer suggest?', 'A stronger bass line.'], ['Must Kai accept every suggestion?', 'No. They can compare versions and decide together.']] },
    choose: [['We can build (on / under) what works.', 'on'], ['A stronger bass line may bring the chorus (out / away).', 'out'], ['I want to stay true (to / of) the song.', 'to'], ['Be open (to / at) feedback.', 'to'], ['Could we (try / tried) another arrangement?', 'try'], ['Feedback does not necessarily (mean / means) replacing everything.', 'mean']],
    choices: [['What is the proposed change?', 'A stronger chorus arrangement.', 'A completely different career for Kai.', 'A'], ['What concerns Kai?', 'Whether the studio has a café.', 'Whether changes will weaken the song’s identity.', 'B'], ['What makes the feedback constructive?', 'It recognises a strength and suggests a focused experiment.', 'It demands agreement without listening.', 'A'], ['What happens after experimentation?', 'Kai rejects every revision.', 'Kai says the revised version sounds better.', 'B']],
    mission: { situation: 'You are Kai. Your tutor is Maya. Listen to her new message after trying arrangements. Confirm what the team changed and what still belongs in the comparison.', task: 'Summarise the revision, explain the artistic concern, and respond to feedback with a clear reason.', roles: ['Maya', 'Kai'], title: 'After the arrangement session', text: 'Kai, we tried different arrangements this afternoon, and you said the revised version sounded better. The aim was to strengthen the chorus while keeping the song’s identity. When we compare it with the earlier recording, listen to the effect of the bass line and how the chorus feels. This was a shared revision, not a decision to turn you into a different artist. Please explain what you want to keep and why.', lines: ['Kai, what was the aim of the arrangement changes?', 'What did you think after hearing the revised version?', 'Which part should we compare with the earlier recording?', 'Does trying our suggestion mean giving up your artistic identity?', 'What would you like to keep, and why?'], evidence: 'Fresh message confirms that the team experimented and Kai liked the revision. Do not claim a released single, a final mastered track or agreement to every future change. The bass suggestion and stronger chorus are about this song, not a real production rule. Accept a specific reason without requiring technical music knowledge.', useful: ['The aim was to ...', 'I want to stay true to ...', 'The change brings out ...', 'I am open to ..., but ...', 'I would keep ... because ...'] },
    topics: [['CREATIVE FEEDBACK', ['How can you recognise a strength before suggesting a change?', 'What makes feedback easier to consider?', 'Why compare versions before deciding?']], ['ARTISTIC IDENTITY', ['How do you explain what matters in your work?', 'Can feedback support an original idea?', 'What would you ask if a suggestion felt too broad?']], ['EXPERIMENTS', ['When is trying an option more useful than arguing about it?', 'How do you decide what to keep?', 'What can a team learn from an unsuccessful version?']]],
    trivia: 'An “arrangement” is how musical parts are organised and presented. Changing an arrangement does not always mean changing the song’s melody or main idea. The surrounding context tells you what kind of revision is proposed.', triviaQuestion: ['Does a new arrangement always mean replacing the whole song?', 'No. It can change how the parts support the existing song.'],
    closing: 'The team experiments with arrangements rather than replacing Kai’s song. Kai likes the revised version. Maya’s feedback supports the music while leaving room for his artistic choices.',
  },
